      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "# Stability control endpoints, routed\n\nThe four endpoints in `api.stability.ai` that do ControlNet-shaped work, wired so the **same image can go through any combination of them without rewiring**.\n\n## The four, and what they actually hold\n\n| Node | Endpoint | Takes structure from | The dial |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Control Sketch | `control/sketch` | the image, read as line art | `control_strength` 0-1 |\n| Control Structure | `control/structure` | the image, read as mass and depth | `control_strength` 0-1 |\n| Control Style | `control/style` | **nothing** | `fidelity` 0-1 |\n| Style Transfer | `control/style-transfer` | `init_image` | three separate dials |\n\n**Read that third row before you use it.** `control/style` does not preserve your image's composition at all \u2014 it takes the image as a *style* reference and generates a new picture from the prompt, at whatever `aspect_ratio` you set. It is the odd one out, and it is the one people wire up expecting structure and then wonder why nothing survived.\n\nStyle Transfer is the only one with a genuinely separable vector: `style_strength` (how much of the reference's look lands), `composition_fidelity` (how hard the init image's composition is held \u2014 raise this when structure drifts), and `change_strength` (how far from the init image it may travel at all).\n\n## How the routing works\n\nEvery stage is preceded by an **Image Switch**, and `select` is the only thing you change:\n\n```\nSwitch 1  ->  Sketch      1=source  2=line map\nSwitch 2  ->  Structure   1=source  2=line map  3=sketch out\nSwitch 3  ->  Style       1=source  2=line map  3=sketch out  4=structure out\nSwitch 4  ->  Transfer    1=source  2=line map  3=sketch out  4=structure out  5=style out\n```\n\n- **All four set to 1** - every endpoint runs off the source independently. This is compare mode: four takes on the same image, side by side.\n- **1, 3, 4, 5** - a serial chain: source into Sketch, its output into Structure, that into Style, that into Transfer.\n- **Anything between** - Structure off the source, Style off Structure's output, Transfer off the source again. Sixteen subsets, and every sub-sequence in this order.\n\n**Order is fixed by the wiring, and that is a real constraint rather than an oversight.** A ComfyUI graph must be acyclic, so a switch can only read from stages *above* it - wiring Structure to read Style's output while Style reads Structure's would be a cycle and the graph would refuse to run. To try a different order, drag the connections. The switches give you every combination; they cannot give you every permutation.\n\n## Cost\n\n**Every un-bypassed stage is one paid call, whether or not you use its output.** A default run is four calls. To drop a stage, select it and press **Ctrl+B** - bypass passes the input straight through to the output, so downstream switches reading it receive whatever went in, and the stage costs nothing. That is the cheap way to explore: bypass three, study one.\n\n## Keep the seed fixed\n\nThe seed widgets are set to **7**, not 0. Stability treats **seed 0 as \"pick a random one\"**, which means two runs are never comparable and nothing is ever served from cache. With a fixed seed the only thing changing between runs is the routing you changed - and every request you have already made comes back from the local cache for free, so re-running after adjusting one downstream dial costs only that stage.\n\n## The line map\n\n`control/sketch` wants line art. **Canny** builds it locally, and **Invert Image** flips Canny's white-on-black to the dark-lines-on-light a sketch endpoint expects. Watch the preview: raise `low_threshold` to drop weak edges, lower it to keep more. Bypass the invert (Ctrl+B) if your source is already a drawing.\n\n## Why this is here at all\n\nThe FLUX.2 API exposes no control parameters - no control strength, no style weight, nothing. These four endpoints are the only place in this pack where structural adherence is a **number you set** rather than a sentence you phrase. If you are trying to feel out what a fidelity dial actually does, do it here, then carry the intuition back to the FLUX.2 side."
      ],
      "title": "Read me \u2014 routing the control endpoints",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
